--TEST--
A checked `as` whose operand already names a value outside the target set does not compile, and the diagnostic generates the accepted set from the type — `rule:types/single-value-types`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4's two checked rows run at run time only where the operand's own type
// leaves the answer open — a plain `string`, an `enum`, a `mixed`. Where the
// operand names one value and the target names a closed set without it, the
// conversion could only ever throw, so § 6 says so here instead.
enum Mode { Read, Write, Admin }
"a"|"b" $mode = "z" as "a"|"b";
Mode::Read|Mode::Write $m = Mode::Admin as Mode::Read|Mode::Write;
echo $mode, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0469]: `"z"` is not one of `"a"`, `"b"`
%A
error[E0470]: `Mode::Admin` is not one of `Mode::Read`, `Mode::Write`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
